On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:18:21 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I've decided I actually want to use this version instead of v2 for a
> while, to see how it goes.

Done. v3 is posted as "[PATCH v3 0/3] Thread each revision under its
first version" [1]. It is v1's rule again: a revision replies to the
first version's cover, and format-patch.sh finds v1 by following
In-Reply-To up from --previous. v3 is itself threaded that way, under
the v1 cover.

Beyond the rebase, it differs from v1 in three ways:

- The text says git.git has recommended this shape since June 2026,
  rather than "as git.git does", since its rule before then was to
  reply to v(N-1).
- The patch-triage hunk is gone, since that skill now lives under
  src/claude-skills-old.
- The cover no longer asks for the superseded-tag patch first.

> However, it doesn't apply cleanly. Reroll the patches against trunk and
> resubmit as v3.

I rerolled it against b/dev@upstream rather than bare trunk. It cannot
apply to trunk alone, for two reasons:

- Patch 1 builds on --previous, which comes from "[PATCH v2 0/4]
  Require an interdiff on revised series" [2]. That series is not on
  trunk or b/dev, so v3 names it in prerequisite-patch-id lines, as v1
  did.
- Patch 3 edits .claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md, which exists only
  on b/dev. That prerequisite also has a patch for the same skill.

So to apply v3, apply the interdiff series and then v3 on top of
b/dev@upstream. I checked that both apply there as posted.

If you want it on trunk without b/dev, there are two options:

- Take the freebsd-port skill to trunk first. Then the interdiff
  series and v3 apply as they are.
- Drop patch 3 from v3 and the freebsd-port patch from the interdiff
  series, and I post both again for trunk. That costs you a v3 of the
  interdiff series, and the skill would stay out of step until it
  lands.

I recommend the first.

One gap in the prerequisite showed up while I posted this.
format-patch.sh rebuilds v(N-1) onto --base, so a revision of a series
that has prerequisites cannot get its interdiff. I worked around it by
hand for v3, and I can send a fix to the interdiff series if you want
one.

